\label{rem:k-linear}
Notice, in \Cref{defn:modA}, if ${\mathcal A}$ is a $\Bbbk$-linear category, for a commutative ring $\Bbbk$, and $F \colon \mathcal{A} \to \mathrm{Ab}$ is finitely presented then $F(X)$ is canonically a $\Bbbk$-module for all $X \in \mathcal{A}$ as it is the cokernel of a $\Bbbk$-linear map of $\Bbbk$-modules. Similarly, any map $F(f) \colon F(Y) \to F(X)$ is $\Bbbk$-linear for any morphism $f \in {\mathcal A}(X,Y)$. Hence, $F$ can be canonically interpreted as a $\Bbbk$-linear functor ${\mathcal A} \to \mathsf{Mod} \Bbbk$. 

With the above structure one can also easily show that any natural transformation $F \to G$ of finitely presented functors $F$ and $G$ becomes $\Bbbk$-linear with respect to this canonical $\Bbbk$-module structure, using the Yoneda lemma.  It follows that we could have replaced $\mathrm{Ab}$ by $\mathsf{Mod} \Bbbk$ in \Cref{defn:modA} and obtain the same category $\opname{mod}\nolimits \mathcal{A}$. In particular, the category of finitely presented functors is a $\Bbbk$-linear category.

