\label{defn:transquiver}
    A \emph{stable translation quiver} is a pair $(Q, \tau)$ consisting of a locally finite quiver $Q = (Q_0, Q_1)$ without double arrows and a bijection $\tau \colon Q_0 \to Q_0$ which satisfies $(\tau x)^+ = x^-$.
    The bijection $\sigma \colon Q_1 \to Q_1$ with $\sigma Q_1(x, y) = Q_1(\tau y, x)$ is called the \emph{polarisation of $(Q,\tau)$}.

\label{defn:quiver}
    Given a $\Bbbk$-linear, $\mathsf{Hom}$-finite, idempotent complete category ${\mathcal A}$ one can define a quiver $Q = (Q_0, Q_1)$ without double arrows, called the \emph{quiver of ${\mathcal A}$}, and a valued quiver $(Q,a)$, called the \emph{valued quiver of ${\mathcal A}$}.
    \begin{enumerate}[label={(\alph*)}]
        \item The vertices of $Q_0$ are isomorphism classes $[X]$ of $X \in \opname{ind} {\mathcal A}$,
        \item there is an arrow $\alpha \in Q_1([X],[Y])$ precisely if $\mathsf{rad}_{{\mathcal A}}(X,Y)/\mathsf{rad}_{{\mathcal A}}^2(X,Y) \neq 0$ for any $X,Y \in \opname{ind} {\mathcal A}$ and in this case $a(\alpha) = \mathsf{dim}_{\Bbbk} \mathsf{rad}_{{\mathcal A}}(X,Y)/\mathsf{rad}_{{\mathcal A}}^2(X,Y)$.
    \end{enumerate}

\label{rem:unique}
    Under the notation and assumptions of \Cref{defn:ARquiver} assume the quiver $Q$ of ${\mathcal C}$ is connected. Then the action of the Auslander--Reiten translation $\tau$ on objects and hence the AR-quiver $(Q,a,\tau)$ of ${\mathcal C}$ does only depend on the underlying $\Bbbk$-linear structure.
    Indeed, the existence of a Serre-functor is independent of the triangulation and by \cite[Proposition I.2.4]{ReitenVandenBergh} any triangulation on ${\mathcal C}$ has Auslander--Reiten triangles. We may assume that $Q$ has more than one vertex because otherwise the action of $\tau$ on objects is unique anyway. Using that $(Q,\tau)$ is a connected stable translation quiver, one then easily shows that at each vertex of $Q$ starts and ends at least one arrow and hence the Auslander--Reiten triangles of ${\mathcal C}$ are non-degenerate. Applying \cite[Theorem 4.9]{shah} shows the claim.

