 \label{T:VdB} 
Let $S \cong \mathbb{C}\llbracket z_1, \ldots, z_n \rrbracket/I$ be a $3$-dimensional Gorenstein \mbox{$\mathbb{C}$-algebra} with an isolated singularity. If $\pi\colon Y \to \mathsf{Spec} S$ is a \emph{small} resolution of singularities, then there is a tilting bundle ${\mathcal T} \in \opname{D}^{\mathrm{b}}(Y)$. In particular, there is a triangle
equivalence
\begin{align}\label{E:TVdB}
\opname{D}^{\mathrm{b}}(Y) \cong \opname{D}^{\mathrm{b}}(\mathsf{End}_Y({\mathcal T})).
\end{align}

\begin{align}\label{E:TVdB}
\opname{D}^{\mathrm{b}}(Y) \cong \opname{D}^{\mathrm{b}}(\mathsf{End}_Y({\mathcal T})).
\end{align}

For the convenience of the reader, we include a sketch of the argument.

We first produce a generator ${\mathcal G}$ of $\opname{D}^{\mathrm{b}}(Y)$ and then `(partially) mutate' it, to get the tilting bundle ${\mathcal T}$. 

Let ${\mathcal L}$ be an ample line bundle on $Y$. Since $\pi$ has fibers of dimension at most $1$, work of Bondal--Van den Bergh (cf.\ \cite[Lemma 3.2.2]{VandenBergh04}) shows that
\begin{align*}
    {\mathcal G}={\mathcal O}_Y \oplus {\mathcal L}
\end{align*}
generates $\opname{D}^{\mathrm{b}}(Y)$. We next study the graded Ext-algebra of ${\mathcal G}$. Combining ${\mathcal O}_Y \cong \omega_Y $ (cf.\ Remark \ref{rem:crepant-res}), with the Grauert--Riemenschneider vanishing theorem shows that 
\begin{align*}
    \mathsf{R}^q\pi_*{\mathcal O}_Y \cong \mathsf{R}^q\pi_*\omega_Y =0
\end{align*}
for $q>0$ which, since $\mathsf{Spec}(S)$ is affine, implies
\begin{align*}
    \mathsf{Ext}^q_Y({\mathcal O}_Y, {\mathcal O}_Y) \cong H^q(Y, {\mathcal O}_Y) \cong H^0(\mathsf{Spec}(S), \mathsf{R}^q\pi_*{\mathcal O}_Y) =0.  
\end{align*}
Since tensoring with a line bundle is an autoequivalence of $\opname{D}^{\mathrm{b}}(Y)$, we also deduce
\begin{align*}
    \mathsf{Ext}^q_Y({\mathcal L}, {\mathcal L}) =0
\end{align*}
for all $q>0$. Now we claim that $\mathsf{Ext}^q_Y({\mathcal O}_Y, {\mathcal L})=0$ for all $q>0$.
Since ${\mathcal L}$ is ample, it is generated by global sections and we get a short exact sequence
\begin{align*}
    0 \to {\mathcal K} \to {\mathcal O}_Y^l \to {\mathcal L} \to 0.
\end{align*}
Applying $\mathsf{Hom}_Y({\mathcal O}_Y, -)$ yields a long exact sequence and using\footnote{Since we have $\mathsf{R}^{>1}\pi_*{\mathcal F} = 0$ for all coherent sheaves ${\mathcal F}$ by our assumption on the fibers of $\pi$, \cite[Chapter III, Corollary 11.2]{hartshorne}.} 
\begin{align} \label{E:ExtVanish}
    \mathsf{Ext}^{>1}_Y({\mathcal O}_Y, {\mathcal F}) \cong H^{>1}(Y, {\mathcal F}) \cong H^0(\mathsf{Spec}(S), \mathsf{R}^{>1}\pi_*{\mathcal F}) =0
\end{align}
for ${\mathcal F}={\mathcal K}$ and ${\mathcal F}= {\mathcal L}$ shows the claim.

Since $\mathsf{Ext}^1_Y({\mathcal L}, {\mathcal O}_Y) \neq 0$ in general, ${\mathcal G}$ is not tilting. However, there is a well-known construction yielding a tilting bundle in this situation. Consider the universal extension
\begin{align} \label{E:univExt}
    0 \to {\mathcal O}_Y^k \to {\mathcal M} \to {\mathcal L} \to 0,
\end{align}
constructed from a minimal set of generators $\varepsilon_1, \ldots, \varepsilon_k$ of the finitely generated $R$-module $\mathsf{Ext}^1_Y({\mathcal L}, {\mathcal O}_Y)$. In particular, this means that the map 
\begin{align*}
    \mathsf{Hom}_Y({\mathcal O}_Y^k, {\mathcal O}_Y) \to \mathsf{Ext}^1_Y({\mathcal L}, {\mathcal O}_Y) \to 0
\end{align*}
induced by \eqref{E:univExt} is surjective. In combination with the Ext-vanishing between ${\mathcal L}$ and ${\mathcal O}_Y$ that we have established above and with 
\begin{align*}
    \mathsf{Ext}^{>1}_Y({\mathcal L}, {\mathcal O}_Y) \cong \mathsf{Ext}^{>1}_Y({\mathcal O}_Y, {\mathcal L}^{-1}) = 0
\end{align*} (by \eqref{E:ExtVanish}),  this implies that 
\begin{align*}
    \mathsf{Ext}^{>0}_Y({\mathcal O}_Y \oplus {\mathcal M}, {\mathcal O}_Y \oplus {\mathcal M}) =0,
\end{align*}
cf.\ e.g.\ \cite[Lemma 3.1]{HillePerling}. Hence, ${\mathcal T} \cong {\mathcal O}_Y \oplus {\mathcal M}$ is tilting, completing the proof.

\begin{align*}
        {\mathcal G}={\mathcal O}_Y \oplus {\mathcal L}
    \end{align*}

\begin{align*}
